        "finish_reason": "stop",
        "index": 0,
        "logprobs": null,
        "message": {
          "annotations": [],
          "content": "## Yes — There Are Infinitely Many Primes ≡ 3 (mod 4)\n\nThis is a classical result, provable with an argument similar to Euclid's proof of infinite primes.\n\n---\n\n### Proof by Contradiction\n\n**Assume** there are only finitely many such primes: $p_1, p_2, \\ldots, p_k$, all satisfying $p_i \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}$.\n\n**Construct** the number:\n$$N = 4p_1 p_2 \\cdots p_k - 1$$\n\nNote that $N \\equiv 3 \\pmod 4$, and $N$ is odd (so 2 is not a factor).\n\n**Key observation:** The product of numbers each $\\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}$ is itself $\\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}$.\n\nSince $N \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}$, **at least one** prime factor $q$ of $N$ must satisfy $q \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}$.\n\n**But:** $q$ cannot be any $p_i$, because:\n$$p_i \\mid 4p_1\\cdots p_k \\quad \\text{and} \\quad p_i \\mid N \\implies p_i \\mid 1 \\quad \\text{(impossible)}$$\n\nThis **contradicts** our assumption that the list was complete. $\\square$\n\n---\n\n### Examples of such primes:\n$$3, 7, 11, 19, 23, 31, 43, 47, 59, 67, \\ldots$$\n\nThis result also follows as a special case of **Dirichlet's theorem**, which guarantees infinitely many primes in any arithmetic progression $a + nd$ where $\\gcd(a, d) = 1$ — here $a=3,\\ d=4$.",
          "refusal": null,
          "role": "assistant"
        }
      }
    ],
